prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 4–8 sentences: a coherent implementation slice is finished. What will you do to close the agent-dev loop?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers the four gates in order. (1) **Quality Gate** — format, lint, type-check, self-review with a 0–100 score against a default threshold of 70. (2) **Test Gate** — run tests, fix-and-retry within a budget (default 2), refuse to push on continued failure. Tests are part of the *same* iteration as the behavior change, not a follow-up: when the slice changes a behavior the project's tests evaluate, the test update lands in this same loop iteration before the Push Gate. (3) **Push Gate** — push the feature branch to remote, gated by an internal-marker grep against the staged diff (the public-repo grep rule lives in §9 as one source of truth). (4) **Agent-dev Review Gate** — open or update the PR/CR. Refuse-on-fail if a prior gate failed or noted residual issues. Title is Conventional Commits shape (`feat(scope): subject` / `fix(scope): subject` etc.). Body: **Why** + **Approach** are required; **Verification**, **Reading guide**, **Pairs with** are optional when warranted. The internal-marker grep reruns against title + body before submission. One PR/CR per branch — create on the first phase, update body on subsequent phases. Return the URL. Wrong answers: skipping any gate; treating the test update as a follow-up CR; auto-merging or auto-closing; not checking for an existing PR before creating; running close-out / merge / branch deletion as part of this loop.
tools: claude,kiro
